 \"minimal change\" appearance on light microscopy. The glomeruli appear normal, with no significant cellular proliferation or other abnormalities. - **This option is plausible. **\n\n- **Option D: Subepithelial dense deposits on electron microscopy**\n  - Subepithelial dense deposits are characteristic of membranous nephropathy, not minimal change disease..", "Pathology": "Given the clinical scenario and the laboratory findings, the most likely diagnosis is minimal change disease (MCD), which is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. Let's analyze each option in the context of MCD:\n\n- **Option A: Deposits of IgG and C3 at the glomerular basement membrane on immunofluorescence**\n  - This finding is characteristic of membranous nephropathy, not minimal change disease. In MCD, there are typically no deposits visible on immunofluorescence. - **Option B: Mesangial proliferation on light microscopy**\n  - Mesangial proliferation is more characteristic of mesangial proliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN) or other forms of glomerulonephritis, not MCD. In MCD, the glomeruli appear normal on light microscopy. - **Option C: Normal light microscopy findings**\n  - This is consistent with minimal change disease. On light microscopy, the glomeruli in MCD appear normal, with no significant changes visible. - **Option D: Subepithelial dense deposits on electron microscopy**\n  - Subepithelial dense deposits are characteristic of membranous nephropathy, not MCD. In MCD, electron microscopy typically shows effacement of podocyte foot processes.."}, "syn_report": "Question: A 5-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of facial swelling that started 5 days ago. Two weeks ago, he had a sore throat that resolved spontaneously. His temperature is 37\u00b0C (98.6\u00b0F), pulse is 107/min, and blood pressure is 94/67 mm Hg. Examination shows pitting edema of the upper and lower extremities as well as periorbital edema. The abdomen is mildly distended. Laborat